              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Liz"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Easy to prepare and my husband gave it a \"thumbs up.\" I find the baking powder taste a little strong; I can taste more baking powder than rhubarb! But, it's a good use for the abundant fresh rhubarb coming out of the garden these days."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Stephanie Custozzo-Barkay"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Made it in a greased, glass 8X8 baking pan. Chopped rhubarb into 1 inch pieces. Cooked perfectly in 40 minutes. Toothpick comes out wet, not doughy. Great contrast between the tart rhubarb and the sweet syrup. Too sweet for me but, my husband loved it. Kinda like a sweet-tart.\nUPDATE I made this with very sour blueberries. The taste was balanced. I used a 6 x 11 pan for more room.",
            "datePublished": "2017-08-07T14:15:25.087Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Pam B"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I just took this out of the oven after 40 minutes. It was done perfectly. of course I had to taste it. The sweetness is nicely complimented by the tartness of the rhubarb. I cut up about 1-1/2 cups rhubarb so used it all. After reading reviews that it was too sweet I cut back on the brown sugar just a smidgen. Baked it in a 1-1/2 quart glass rectangular pan. I'm planning to put a scoop of vanilla ice cream on it a little later. Looking forward to it.",
            "datePublished": "2018-06-11T15:37:56.640Z"
          },
